package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setWildsOfEldraine = StorySection(
    "woe", "Wilds of Eldraine",
    "Months after the Phyrexians fell, Eldraine still lay under a curse of endless sleep, and a half fae boy from a village of sheep was sent into the Wilds to break it.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-the-set", "The Set",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-a-tale-only-you", "A Tale Only You Can Tell",
                    listOf(
                        "Wilds of Eldraine came out on September 8, 2023, under the set code WOE. Prerelease weekends ran the week before, and the digital release went live on September 5. It was the ninety seventh expansion in the history of the game, a set of 266 cards, and its tagline promised a tale only you can tell. Its expansion symbol is an open storybook with a diamond nestled in its pages, which is as good a summary of the set as any.",
                        "It sits at a hinge in the story. The Phyrexian [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) had ended a few months before, in March of the Machine and its Aftermath, and Wilds of Eldraine was the first set of the three year arc that followed, the one built around the [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths), the doors between worlds that the invasion left hanging open. After it came The Lost Caverns of Ixalan, and the boy whose story this set tells walked straight from one into the other.",
                        "It also marked a change to Standard. No sets rotated out of the format when Wilds of Eldraine arrived, and from then on sets were meant to stay legal for three years instead of two. For a set that leans so hard on enchantments that stick around and keep doing things, that was a fitting time to land.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-back-to-eldraine", "Back to Eldraine",
                    listOf(
                        "Eldraine had been visited once before, in Throne of Eldraine in 2019, where the focus was the Realm, its five knightly courts and a world shaped by the legends of King Arthur. The return went the other way. Wilds of Eldraine looks past the castles into the Wilds, the dark fae forests beyond the edge of the Realm, and it leans much harder on fairy tales than on Arthurian legend, though the knights are still there. The lead designer was Chris Mooney and the lead developer Ian Duke, with Mark Rosewater among the vision designers.",
                        "Where the first visit had themes for both artifacts and enchantments, this one leaned toward enchantments. Each of the ten two colour draft archetypes was tied to a single fairy tale and told on its cards from beginning to end, so that a drafter in blue and black was playing out Sleeping Beauty while a drafter in black and red was playing the Pied Piper. Five of those tales belong to the main story, and the other five stand on their own. The [Ten Tales](woe-ten-tales) tab tells the five that stand alone.",
                        "The set has only one planeswalker card, {Ashiok, Wicked Manipulator}, and Wizards said around this time that one planeswalker per set was the plan going forward. That suited the story. Most planeswalkers had lost their sparks in the weeks after the invasion, the Kenrith twins among them, and a world full of heroes with no way off it was exactly the kind of world a fairy tale could be set in. The Story Spotlight cards, which show the key moments of the official story in art, include {Into the Fae Court}, {Feed the Cauldron}, {Up the Beanstalk}, {Kellan's Lightblades}, {Break the Spell}, {Twisted Fealty} and {Extraordinary Journey}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-in-the-packs", "In the Packs",
                    listOf(
                        "Wilds of Eldraine came in Draft Boosters, Set Boosters, Collector Boosters, a Bundle and two Commander decks. There were no Jumpstart Boosters this time. A Starter Kit took their place, and the new Jumpstart cards made for the set were folded into Set and Collector Boosters instead. Every Draft, Set and Collector Booster also carried at least one card from Enchanting Tales, a bonus sheet of sixty three reprinted enchantments in a frame of their own, which has its own [tab](woe-enchanting-tales) on this page.",
                        "The special treatments were all storybook. Twenty cards came in a storybook showcase frame, with art made to look like the illustrations of an old book of tales. There were borderless cards and extended art cards, and five full art fairy tale basic lands painted by Hari and Deepti, which turned up in the land slot of about a third of draft and set boosters. Set Boosters could also hold Universes Within versions of the characters from the Walking Dead Secret Lair, renamed and redrawn to fit Magic's own worlds.",
                        "The set's promos carried the same flavour. The prerelease card was a stamped rare or mythic from the set. The Buy a Box card was an alternate art {Expel the Interlopers}, and the Bundle held a foil alternate art {Lich-Knights' Conquest}. At Store Championships the winner took home a textless {Moonshaker Cavalry}. Even the tokens were part of the storybook. The new Role tokens were printed two to a card, one upside down beneath the other, and the art for the Cursed and Wicked Roles came out swapped, which Mark Rosewater confirmed was a misprint.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Story", "A First Look at Wilds of Eldraine, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Collecting Wilds of Eldraine, by Mike Turian, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds Story, Parts 1 and 2, by Mark Rosewater, Making Magic on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-the-plane", "The Plane",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-fallen-courts", "The Fallen Courts",
                    listOf(
                        "Long ago the elves ruled Eldraine, and humans rose against them and drove them into the forests. On the land they won, the humans founded five knightly courts, each built around a relic of great power and unknown origin, and each devoted to one virtue. Ardenvale held Loyalty, Vantress Knowledge, Locthwain Persistence, Embereth Courage and Garenbrig Strength. Together they were the Realm, and in the generation before the invasion they were ruled by High King Algenus Kenrith and Queen Linden.",
                        "The invasion broke all of it. Within days the courts had fallen, the High King and Queen were dead, and Queen Ayara of Locthwain had been compleated along with her guards. Castle Ardenvale, the seat of the High King, stood abandoned until a witch moved in. Many of Embereth's knights and nearly all of Locthwain's were killed. King Yorvo, the giant who ruled Garenbrig, escaped the ruin of his court and ended up a guest in the clouds. Much of the surviving population fled into the Wilds, the very place the Realm had been built to keep out.",
                        "What was left gathered around Vantress, the court of Knowledge, whose castle floats and drifts across the Realm. It became the seat of Will Kenrith, who ruled as acting king. Its relic, the magic mirror called Indrelon, was no longer there. It had been stolen and carried up into the sky. Of the other courts, only scraps remained, and some knights stopped pretending otherwise.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "While the realm's great minds quarreled over the strange eclipse, Locthwain fell to the invaders.",
                        "Phyrexian Unlife, Enchanting Tales, quoting Last Stand of the Realm",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-knights-without-courts", "Knights Without Courts",
                    listOf(
                        "Some knights who survived the invasion gave up on the courts altogether. Knights from all five came together as the Verdant Order, renounced their old oaths and swore instead to defend the last untouched parts of the Wilds. They patrol the forests along the edge of the Realm and turn back loggers and hunters, and though they no longer serve a court, they still hold to the five virtues. {Verdant Outrider} shows one of them on patrol.",
                        "Locthwain's survivors went a harder way. With their queen compleated and their order nearly wiped out, many of them now sell their swords as mercenaries or wander as knights errant, no longer bound by the code they swore to. Locthwain's relic, the Cauldron of Eternity, which could return the worthy dead to life, had vanished, and people say that those who go looking for it now hope to undo what the invasion did.",
                        "Others took up gentler work. {Woodland Acolyte} shows a knight who left knighthood behind to heal the scars of the invasion. And there were knights who simply slept. When the curse spread it did not spare the knights who had fought the Phyrexians, and the cards remember them still dreaming. {Slumbering Keepguard} draws strength from dreams of a realm united, and the sleeping people of Ardenvale dream of a tireless protector standing guard over their homes, the knight on {Savior of the Sleeping}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Some knights who survived the invasion have forsaken what remains of their courts. The newly formed Verdant Order has sworn to defend the last untouched parts of the wilds.",
                        "Verdant Outrider",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-boundary-lands", "The Boundary Lands",
                    listOf(
                        "Between the Realm and the Wilds lies a vague border country called the Boundary Lands, crossed by a cart road and frequented by humans and fae alike, who trade with each other for coin. Orrinshire is out here, a village so remote that the yearly visit of a single travelling merchant is a holiday in itself. It is best known for its wool, and it has at least five times as many sheep as people. Its folk are wary of the fae and of anyone touched by them, which made it a hard place to grow up for a boy with a fae father.",
                        "Edgewall is the busier town, the last stop before the forest. Beneath it lie old dwarven ruins and sewers, and in the sewers ruled a demonic rat called Lord Skitter. Not far from Edgewall the land gives way to Dunbarrow, a misty marsh of twisted trees, home to witches and giant spiders, where the first of the three witch sisters kept her cabin.",
                        "Outside Locthwain the invasion left a wound in the ground. The Tanglespan is a great chasm the defenders of Eldraine opened to trap the invaders, and the fiery Torbran was sent to lure the Phyrexians into it. Now it is crossed by a web of bridges and beanstalks and lived in by fauns and trolls. {Tanglespan Lookout} keeps watch from its heights, and {Graceful Takedown} shows the fauns using its ledges against bigger foes.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-deep-wilds", "The Deep Wilds",
                    listOf(
                        "The Wilds are where the Realm's rules stop. Their borders shift, and distance, direction, time and the seasons all bend inside them. The people of the Realm call their inhabitants the fair folk, faeries and elves, undines and redcaps, witches who keep to themselves, and the dwarves and giants who chose not to live among humans. Most of the Wilds is endless woodland, dotted with crumbling forts and overgrown castles, and the closest thing it has to a government is a Council of Druids, elves who would like the old days of elven rule back.",
                        "Deep inside lies Tuinvale, home of the High Fae. Its court is a palace of hewn stone on ground covered in cool moss and silver grass. Its buildings shift and glimmer as if they were half made of glass, from thatched houses as big as mountains to tiny castles full of tiny knights, and its twisted trees bear jewelled fruit. A visitor is invited in by the appearance of an archway of pale translucent stone. Elsewhere in the Wilds stand Redtooth Keep, one of the last elven citadels, and Sweettooth Village, abandoned and overrun by monsters made of sweets.",
                        "Out past Dunbarrow lies Loch Larent, a lake where people used to go fishing and where the merfolk lived under their leader Sharae. When the witch Hylda built her palace of ice above it, she froze the whole loch, and eerie blue lights drifted under its frozen surface. Her castle was called Winter's Home, a main tower on a rocky cliff over the loch, reached by a road of glittering crystal gravel and five portcullises with drawbridges leading nowhere. The heaps of the fallen at the outer drawbridge showed how far most adventurers got.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Tarry not in Tuinvale, / Ignore the flowers' heady scents. / Take no thing, stay to the trail, / Lest the fae seek recompense.",
                        "Bitterblossom, Enchanting Tales, quoting To Walk the Wilds",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-kingdom-of-storms", "The Kingdom of Storms",
                    listOf(
                        "Above the Wilds floats the Kingdom of Storms, a realm of storm giants built of cottages, keeps and towers spread across islands of magical cloud. The only way up is to climb one of the vast beanstalks called Everstalks. Besides the giants, it is home to other creatures of giant size, geese and oxen and elemental elk called tempest harts, and to hunters who think a cloud eater or a beanstalk wurm is the only prey worthy of a giant's appetite.",
                        "Its great castle is Stormkeld, a massive marble keep in the clouds, home to the giant noble Beluna Grandsquall. Beluna is a collector, which is a polite way of saying she steals things, everything from priceless artifacts to whole historic buildings, and her vault is full of them. Indrelon, the magic mirror of Vantress, ended up there. Her pet beanstalk wurms have a habit of escaping, and when they do they plant themselves in the world below. Villages fall and new Everstalks rise, which is where the beanstalks come from in the first place.",
                        "When Kellan needed to find a witch nobody could find, the mirror in Beluna's vault was his only lead, so up the beanstalk he went. The [Kellan's Quest](woe-kellans-quest) tab tells how that went, on the day of Beluna's birthday, with the exiled king of Garenbrig at her table.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "When Beluna's pets escape Stormkeld, villages fall and new Everstalks rise.",
                        "Beanstalk Wurm",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Set", "Enchanting Tales"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episodes 1 to 5, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Eldraine, The Adventures of Rankle, Master of Love, by Jenna Helland, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-kellans-quest", "Kellan's Quest",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-wicked-slumber", "The Wicked Slumber",
                    listOf(
                        "The High Fae had been away when the Phyrexians came, off on a long journey to the edge of the plane with their lord, Talion. They came home to find their world being eaten. Talion made a deal they would not otherwise have made, with three witch sisters named Agatha, Hylda and Eriette, and together they cast a curse over all of Eldraine. It was the Wicked Slumber, and the Phyrexians fell asleep where they stood. On the edge of the Tanglespan, the trap Eldraine had dug for them closed at the same moment, and the invasion of Eldraine was over.",
                        "For their help Talion paid each witch with a gift that swelled her power. Agatha got a soul cauldron that could boil living things down to their magical essence. Hylda got a crown of winter that could call blizzards, snow and ice. Eriette got a charmed glass apple. But when the Phyrexians were gone, the curse did not lift. It spread instead, to the living, and grew stronger. Farmers, knights and fae fell asleep wherever they stood, Talion's own daughter Obyra among them, and creatures that feed on dreams came to graze on the sleepers.",
                        "Will Kenrith, ruling as acting king, ordered that every sleeper be laid in a glass casket, protected and watched in case they ever woke, while the wizards of Vantress studied the curse. He and his twin Rowan had lost the spark they shared in the weeks after the invasion, one more loss among the [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost). Six months on, the Realm was coming apart. Will met the knight Imodane in single combat, and Rowan's lightning cut a rift so deep into a mountain that it has been called Stormcutter Mountain ever since.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The king ordered those lost in sleep be placed in glass caskets, protected and watched in case they might one day wake.",
                        "Rest in Peace, Enchanting Tales, quoting The Wicked Slumber",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-fae-lords-bargain", "The Fae Lord's Bargain",
                    listOf(
                        "In Orrinshire lived a sixteen year old boy named Kellan. His mother Alyse was human and had married a shepherd named Ronald, but Kellan's father had been one of the fae, and everyone in the village knew it. The other young people roughed him up for it, and some of them blamed him for the Slumber itself. He dreamed of being a hero anyway.",
                        "One day he stepped out of his village into a strange world where the trees bore jewelled fruit. It was the court of Tuinvale, and Talion, the Kindly Lord, wanted to know whether he was true of heart. Talion had already sent the knight Imodane to kill the three witches, and Agatha had caught her. So the fae lord turned to the boy instead, with a quest. Kellan was to fetch back the three gifts Talion had given the witch sisters, the cauldron, the crown and the apple, and with them break the Slumber. His reward would be the truth about the father he had never known.",
                        "Talion gave him two magical basket hilts with no blades in them, and sent him off. The card {Into the Fae Court} shows the moment Kellan first stood before the throne, and {Talion, the Kindly Lord} shows the one who sent him. A boy who wanted to be a hero had been handed a quest by a lord who was not known for kindness, whatever their title said, and he said yes.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Kellan stepped from his village into a strange world where trees bore jeweled fruit. A regal, inhuman voice rang in his ears: \"Tell me, brave hero, are you true of heart?\"",
                        "Into the Fae Court",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-vile-cauldron", "The Vile Cauldron",
                    listOf(
                        "In the streets of Edgewall Kellan met Ruby, a tough young scout in a red hood who would do anything for her family. Her brother Peter had gone missing, and she agreed to help Kellan find the local witch in the hope that the witch might lead her to him. Neither of them knew that Agatha had already taken Peter and bound him with her magic to be her muscle, a knight in the shape of a wolf.",
                        "The road through the Boundary Lands into Dunbarrow was a string of dangers. The Wolf Knight hunted them through the forest, and they only got away by running straight through a pack of witchstalkers, wolves drawn to the stink of dark magic, which turned on the armoured figure instead of them. {Witchstalker Frenzy} shows that escape. At last they came to Agatha's cabin. Agatha looked kind and motherly, and it was a disguise over something bestial. Inside they found Imodane, still her prisoner.",
                        "Kellan fought the witch and slammed into her, and she fell into her own cauldron. {Agatha of the Vile Cauldron} died in it, and Imodane went free. With Agatha dead, her hold on the Wolf Knight broke, and the beast that had chased them through the forest turned back into Ruby's brother. Kellan carried the cauldron back as proof, and in the tavern tellings that followed, he got taller and Ruby got stronger with every retelling, which is the joke on {A Tale for the Ages}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Kellan heard Agatha howl when he slammed into her, heard her scream as she fell into the cauldron, but couldn't afford to think about the implications just yet.",
                        "Feed the Cauldron",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-up-the-beanstalk", "Up the Beanstalk",
                    listOf(
                        "The second witch was harder to find. Hylda had hidden herself away, and the only thing on Eldraine that might know where was Indrelon, the magic mirror of Vantress, which had been stolen and carried into the clouds. For a guide Kellan and Ruby took on Troyan, a dashing blue skinned vedalken from a city far away, who had come to Eldraine through an Omenpath looking for adventure and who called himself an expert climber.",
                        "Troyan led them up a beanstalk into the Kingdom of Storms. They slipped past giant guards and storm beasts and ended up in the parlour of the storm giants on the worst possible day, Beluna Grandsquall's birthday. Beluna was slow to trust them, since outsiders might be after her many precious treasures, never mind that she had stolen every one of them first. It was Yorvo, the exiled king of Garenbrig, who talked her into letting them into her vault to ask the mirror.",
                        "The mirror showed them the second witch, in a fortress of ice she had built over Loch Larent, and they set off at once. Meanwhile, far below, Rowan Kenrith was sitting down to a very different banquet, at a table in Castle Ardenvale with a witch who called herself a queen. The [Witch Queen](woe-the-witch-queen) tab picks up both threads.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"It's not so bad. All you have to do is keep from looking down.\" —Troyan, to Kellan and Ruby",
                        "Up the Beanstalk",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episode 1, Pure of Heart, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episode 2, Wandering Knight, Budding Hero, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episode 3, Two Great Banquets, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Ten Stories Tall, by Mark Rosewater, Making Magic on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Wicked Slumber"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Kellan"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-the-witch-queen", "The Witch Queen",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-frozen-heart", "The Frozen Heart",
                    listOf(
                        "Hylda was the coldest of the three sisters, as aloof as the blizzards she commanded. With the crown Talion had given her she had raised a kingdom of ice at the far edge of Dunbarrow and frozen Loch Larent to make room for her palace. Some of the merfolk dove deep enough to escape the chill, and some were not so lucky. Reindeer herds moved in, drawn by the quiet. Every day she wore the crown, her kingdom spread and her heart grew colder.",
                        "Kellan and Ruby came to Winter's Home looking for the crown, and frost warriors rose out of the ice to stop them. It was there, fighting for his life against Hylda's elemental guards, that Kellan finally found out what Talion's empty hilts were for. Something in him felt bright as spring, poured into them, and formed blades of light, sharp as thorns. {Kellan's Lightblades} and {Kindled Heroism} both show that moment. But the cold was too much, and Kellan collapsed short of the gates. Ruby dragged him the rest of the way through the snow.",
                        "Watching her do it, Hylda felt something she had not felt in a long time, which was compassion. She did not kill them. She kissed Kellan's forehead, and her blessing kept the two of them safe from the cold. Then she took off the crown of winter and gave it up. With it she gave them the truth of the curse. Talion had not only paid the witches to cast it. Talion had cast it with them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As Hylda watched Ruby drag Kellan's unconscious form through the cold, Hylda felt something she hadn't felt in a long time: compassion.",
                        "Break the Spell",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-ardent-queen", "The Ardent Queen",
                    listOf(
                        "While Kellan crossed the Wilds, Rowan Kenrith was looking for a cure of her own. She had trained as a mage at Strixhaven and believed that with enough power she could put everything back the way it was. She enlisted Royse, a spidery fae weaver, and in an abandoned castle she dreamed of her dead parents, who urged her to go home to Castle Ardenvale. When she got there, it was not empty.",
                        "The last sister, Eriette, had claimed the ruined castle and named herself the Ardent Queen. She was vain and controlling, and with the apple Talion had given her she kept the Slumber going long after the Phyrexians were gone. Ranks of sleeping Ardenvale knights filled her throne room, ready to fight for her without a thought in their heads, and she moved the sleepers like puppets. Beside her stood Ashiok, a planeswalker who still had a spark and who used the nightmares of the sleeping against those still awake.",
                        "At a banquet in the castle the two of them told Rowan things she had not known. Her spark was gone. And Eriette was her aunt, sister to the witch who had borne the twins and killed them as newborns before Queen Linden brought them back. Eriette offered her niece what she wanted most, power and the means to keep Eldraine safe. Rowan took it and became her apprentice, learning sleep magic and how to command the sleepers, which is the moment {Twisted Fealty} shows.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Ranks of sleeping Ardenvale knights filled Eriette's throne room, ready to defend their witch-queen with unthinking devotion.",
                        "Eerie Interference",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-broken-oaths", "Broken Oaths",
                    listOf(
                        "Kellan and Ruby came to Ardenvale for the apple, and it was Rowan who met them. She warned them to go home, and when they would not, she knocked them out. They were her prisoners when Will came for the castle with knights from across the Realm, and the sleepers rose to fight for their queen. Will's knights had been hardened by the invasion, and they did not give the nightmares the fear they fed on.",
                        "In the chaos Kellan and Ruby got free. When Ashiok appeared, Kellan did not try to beat the planeswalker alone. He threw himself at Ashiok and held them in place just long enough for Will to land a bolt of ice square in their chest. The knights took Eriette prisoner, though Rowan escaped. The curse was broken, and across Eldraine the sleepers began to wake.",
                        "Kellan went to Talion one last time and turned down his reward. He did not want the truth from a lord who had lied to him. He went home and asked his mother, and Alyse told him that his father was Oko, a fae planeswalker. Eriette did not stay in her cell long. Someone wearing Ashiok's face came for her and freed her, and it was not Ashiok at all but [Jace Beleren](who/jace), who took her off the plane to a heist on [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction). And one day in the woods Kellan found an Omenpath standing among the trees like a cloudy mirror. He had no spark and did not need one. He stepped through to look for his father, and {Extraordinary Journey} shows him going. The whole story is also told in the Lore section's [Wilds of Eldraine](wilds-of-eldraine) article.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Hardened by the Phyrexian invasion, Will's knights starved the nightmares of the thing they craved most: fear.",
                        "Moment of Valor",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episode 3, Two Great Banquets, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episode 4, Ruby and the Frozen Heart, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episode 5, Broken Oaths, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Eriette"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Hylda"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-ten-tales", "Ten Tales",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-swarm-piper", "The Swarm Piper",
                    listOf(
                        "The black and red cards tell the Pied Piper. Totentanz was a struggling musician in Edgewall who could barely make ends meet, and his desperation led him somewhere dark. Sleeping out on the streets one night, he fell into the sewers beneath the town, and the rats led him to their master. Lord Skitter was a demonic rat, cruel and cunning, who lived in the old dwarven ruins under Edgewall, and he told the minstrel that the human world would fall and asked whether he meant to fall with it.",
                        "Skitter gave Totentanz what his music had never had, the power to enthral anyone who heard it, and a magic pipe to play it on. In exchange Totentanz was to lure the people of Edgewall down into the sewers to feed Skitter's brood. He did, by the droves. The townsfolk tapped their feet and hummed along, not knowing they were helping the tune that would doom them, and the rats grew fat and bold until they swarmed the town. Even the hounds the mayor sent after Skitter were bought off with a handful of bones.",
                        "At first the rats outwitted everyone. In the end the people of Edgewall banded together, stole the pipe, stormed the sewers and turned its magic back on Lord Skitter and Totentanz, and the two of them were devoured by their own horde. Without a leader, the rats went back to being ordinary rats. Nobody is quite sure Lord Skitter is gone for good. {Totentanz, Swarm Piper}, {Lord Skitter, Sewer King} and {Song of Totentanz} carry the tale.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "'The human world will fall,' whispered the monstrous rat. 'You have but one choice to make: Will you fall with it?'",
                        "Oppression, Enchanting Tales, quoting Song of the Vermin",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-armont-and-the-beast", "Armont and the Beast",
                    listOf(
                        "Green and white tell Beauty and the Beast, with the roles turned around. Lady Armont was a knight captain of Ardenvale, famed for her kindness and her beauty, and she could not walk past an injustice. Looking for a way to heal the Realm after the invasion, she led her band of loyal knights deep into the Wilds, and there, by chance, she came upon Redtooth Keep, where an ancient bloodline of elves still lived.",
                        "The elves of Redtooth were cursed. Every night they turned into feral werefoxes, and some of Armont's knights, worried for their own skins, fought them against her wishes. Armont gave herself instead to solving the mystery of the curse. Yenna, the proud and distrustful leader of the werefoxes, warmed to her kindness in time and told her of an enchanted rose that could break the spell, a rose that blooms in moonlight in a nearby glade, guarded by an archon.",
                        "Armont and Yenna went to the glade together in search of it. The rose bloomed, and the curse of Redtooth Keep withered at last, which is how the MTG Wiki records that Syr Armont broke it. {Syr Armont, the Redeemer}, {Yenna, Redtooth Regent}, {Curse of the Werefox} and {Archon of the Wild Rose} tell the story, and in the game the archon turns every enchanted creature into something worth fearing.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "And as the rose began to bloom, the curse of Redtooth Keep began, at last, to wither.",
                        "Defense of the Heart, Enchanting Tales, quoting Curse of the Werefox",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-apprentices-folly", "The Apprentice's Folly",
                    listOf(
                        "Blue and red tell the Sorcerer's Apprentice. Johann was the apprentice of the great wizard Gadwick, in Gadwick's tower of Caervelin in Vantress. When his master left on some mysterious wizard business, Johann was left in charge of the tower's many complicated mechanisms. It did not go well. Before long he had let loose a flood of elementals all over the tower, and worse, the fire elementals and the water elementals started fighting each other.",
                        "Johann tried everything. He tried bottling the elements, which is {Johann's Stopgap}. He researched how to beat them, and found out that draconic fire really does dispel rainwater elementals, and then needed a way to dispel the draconic fire. He animated the furniture to fight back, which gave the world {Living Lectern} and a self loading catapult that had seemed like such a good idea. Even the library decided to reorganise itself, and he had to admit that keeping the cookbooks in the cauldron made a certain sense.",
                        "Nothing worked. In the end Johann had to leap out of a window, saved only by the one water spell he had actually mastered, as the tower behind him exploded in a spectacular battle between fire and water. {Torch the Tower} shows the finale. Caervelin burned down, and somewhere Gadwick was presumably going to have something to say about it. {Johann, Apprentice Sorcerer} is the hero, if that is the word.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"Okay. First I'll calm the elementals. Then I'll put out the fire. Then I'll unflood the basement. Then I'll do the chores myself.\"",
                        "Johann, Apprentice Sorcerer",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-sweettooth", "Sweettooth",
                    listOf(
                        "Black and green tell Hansel and Gretel, and it is the darkest of the ten. When they were young, Greta and her brother were lured to Sweettooth Village by a trail of treats, the way children in these stories always are. There they found a slew of vicious creatures made of sweets, mint and pastry and candy with teeth, and the witch behind them. Greta escaped. Her brother did not.",
                        "She grew up into a witch hunter, and she went back. Greta stormed the village with an axe in her hand, smashing candy horrors left and right, looking for the witch behind the whole thing. The village itself was long abandoned, overrun by its sweets, and whoever once had them under control had lost it long ago. The witch, for her part, offered her pie and said her brother had helped to make it.",
                        "While she fought, Greta found a young boy trapped in the village, just as she and her brother had once been trapped. She rescued him and got him out, and saved someone the way nobody had saved her brother. {Greta, Sweettooth Scourge}, {Welcome to Sweettooth}, {Sweettooth Witch} and {Back for Seconds} tell it, and in the game Greta turns every Food token into fuel.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"Try some pie! Your brother helped make it, and he really poured his heart into it.\"",
                        "Sweettooth Witch",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-grand-ball", "The Grand Ball",
                    listOf(
                        "Red and white tell Cinderella. Ash was a refugee from Embereth who lived at Delverhaugh, the underground fortress of the dwarves in Vantress, where her adoptive parents worked as blacksmiths. She had always wanted to go to the Grand Ball at Delverhaugh, but humans were rarely allowed. So she made a plan to disguise herself in armour and sneak in, and the mice of the forges helped her get the armour.",
                        "At the ball she was greeted by Goddric, a handsome and elegant man who asked her to dance, and who was really a dragon in disguise. Then redcaps and ogres attacked. The dwarves fought back with anything they could reach, skewers and pies and whatever was on the table, and the survivors were dubbed the Gluttoneers for killing dozens of redcaps with fruit and cakes. Ash leapt in to defend Goddric from a redcap, and he had no choice but to reveal what he was and drive the invaders out in a blast of dragonfire. That is {Expel the Interlopers}.",
                        "At midnight Ash had to leave, and in her hurry she left her helmet behind. It clattered across the floor and came to a stop while the whole party stared, and the only sound was her footsteps running away. Perhaps the handsome stranger would use it to track her down. {Ash, Party Crasher}, {Goddric, Cloaked Reveler} and {Stroke of Midnight} tell the tale.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The assembled partygoers fell silent and stared. As the helmet came to a clattering stop, the only sound was Ash's distant footsteps.",
                        "Stroke of Midnight",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Set", "Enchanting Tales"),
                StorySource("Story", "Ten Stories Tall, by Mark Rosewater, Making Magic on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Totentanz"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Greta"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-characters", "Characters",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-kellan-and-ruby", "Kellan and Ruby",
                    listOf(
                        "Kellan is the heart of the set. He is a human with fae blood, sixteen when the story begins, raised in Orrinshire by his mother Alyse and his stepfather Ronald, and mocked by the village for a father nobody would name. He wanted to be a hero badly enough to take a quest from the most dangerous being in the Wilds. Over the story he killed a witch, won a crown without a fight, held a planeswalker still long enough to be beaten, and refused a reward from the lord who had lied to him.",
                        "{Kellan, the Fae-Blooded} is a red double striker whose white adventure side, Birthright Boon, goes looking for an Aura or Equipment, and the more he carries, the harder his whole army hits. That is the fairy tale hero as a card, a boy made dangerous by the gifts other people hand him. His blades of light, the empty hilts from Talion that only woke at Winter's Home, show up again on {Kellan's Lightblades}.",
                        "Ruby is the other half of the quest, a tough, street smart young woman in a red hood who would do anything for her family. She signed on to find her brother Peter and ended up fighting him, dragging an unconscious Kellan through the snow, and storming a castle. {Ruby, Daring Tracker} is a red and green legend who taps for mana and grows when a big creature is beside her, which is how she spent the whole story.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Neither the sharpest thorns nor the foulest beasts would stop her from finding her brother.",
                        "Ruby, Daring Tracker",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-kenrith-twins", "The Kenrith Twins",
                    listOf(
                        "Will and Rowan Kenrith had been planeswalkers, sharing a single spark, until it went out in the weeks after the invasion. Both came home to an Eldraine with its High King and Queen dead, and both wanted the same thing, their home made whole. They could agree on nothing else. Will took up his father's sword and sought peace, ruling as acting king from Vantress. Rowan, trained at Strixhaven, believed that enough power could put everything back the way it had been.",
                        "The set draws them as mirror images. {Will, Scion of Peace} is white and blue, a vigilant wizard whose spells grow cheaper the more life you have gained this turn. {Rowan, Scion of War} is black and red, with menace, and her spells grow cheaper the more life you have lost. Each twin has picked up a colour on the way, and their power and toughness are reversed. Not everyone thought Will was fit to rule. Imodane, the knight who met him in single combat, carries a line on her own card saying the realm needs a ruler, not a boy playing king.",
                        "Rowan's road ran through Castle Ardenvale, where her aunt Eriette offered her power and she took it, learning to command the sleepers. When her brother came for the castle she stood on the wrong side of the wall, and when it fell she escaped. Her line on her card says exactly what she wanted, to rebuild Eldraine herself, the old way, with power.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"It's easy to cry 'For glory!' and charge those who disagree with you. But it takes finesse to turn enemies into allies rather than corpses.\"",
                        "Will, Scion of Peace",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-talion-and-obyra", "Talion and Obyra",
                    listOf(
                        "Talion is the leader of the High Fae, the Kindly Lord of Tuinvale, ancient, curious, supremely powerful and utterly otherworldly. Talion uses they and them. They wear black robes, delicately pointed boots and a crown, carry a hawthorn wand, and have piercing golden eyes. They regard themselves as the protector of all of Eldraine's people, and they are given to strange whims, journeying to the farthest edges of the plane out of a curiosity that never seems to run out.",
                        "They are also the reason the Slumber exists. Talion made the bargain with the three witches, paid them the gifts that made them dangerous, and cast the curse with them. Then they sent a boy to undo it without telling him that part. {Talion, the Kindly Lord} names a number as it enters and punishes any opponent who casts a spell matching it, a fae bargain in card form.",
                        "Talion's daughter Obyra is the greatest duelist on Eldraine, and the Slumber took her too. It did not stop her. Her sword fighting instincts run so deep that she sleepwalks across the Wilds hunting for worthy opponents, and her attendants, Beauty, Song and Blessing, chase after her trying to keep her out of trouble while she slashes at them, unseeing. {Obyra, Dreaming Duelist} and {Faerie Fencing} show her at work.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "They are led by Talion, the Kindly Lord, First and Greatest of the High Fae, Sovereign of the Court, the Liege of Laughter, Finder of the First Yew, and the One True Lord of Eldraine.",
                        "Omniscience, Enchanting Tales, quoting On the High Fae",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-three-sisters", "The Three Sisters",
                    listOf(
                        "Agatha, Hylda and Eriette were witch sisters, and each got a gift from Talion that made her far worse. Agatha wore a kind and motherly face over a bestial nature. With her soul cauldron she set up near Edgewall to trick passers by into becoming her next meal, and she befriended the beasts of the Wilds until some of the gentlest grew a taste for flesh. {Agatha's Soul Cauldron} is her gift as a card, and it lets your creatures borrow the abilities of the dead.",
                        "Hylda was the cold one, as aloof as her blizzards. {Hylda's Crown of Winter} taps down whatever stands in her way, and {Hylda of the Icy Crown} rewards you every time an enemy creature is tapped, with an ice elemental, a counter for your whole army, or a fresh card. She is also the only one of the three who showed mercy. She spared Kellan and Ruby at her own gates, gave up her crown of her own will, and told them who had really cast the curse.",
                        "Eriette was the vain and controlling one, and the one who kept the curse going. She took Castle Ardenvale and a niece for an apprentice, and she held a court of sleepers. {Eriette of the Charmed Apple} keeps enchanted creatures from attacking you and drains every opponent for each Aura you control, and {Eriette's Tempting Apple} steals a creature for a turn. She was captured, and then freed by someone wearing Ashiok's face, which is how she ended up in the next set's heist.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"Hush now. I need your beauty sleep.\"",
                        "Eriette of the Charmed Apple",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-ashiok", "Ashiok",
                    listOf(
                        "Ashiok is a sculptor of nightmares, one of the few planeswalkers whose spark survived. They came to Eldraine to profit from a world where everyone was asleep, and a world of sleepers is a banquet for something that feeds on bad dreams. Ashiok stood beside Eriette at Castle Ardenvale and used the nightmares of the sleeping against those still awake, and the dream realm filled with amalgams of childhood terrors feeding on everyone trapped in the Slumber.",
                        "The young noble Neva shows what that meant. When the Slumber took her she was sealed in a glass casket while the mages of Vantress worked to find a cure, and while her body lay safe, her mind wandered the dream realm, stalked by nightmares, some of them shaped like the Phyrexians who had hunted her when she was awake. She survived only because of a mysterious spectral visitor who saved her life. {Neva, Stalked by Nightmares} and {Glass Casket} tell her side.",
                        "{Ashiok, Wicked Manipulator} is the set's only planeswalker. It turns life payments into cards exiled from your library, makes nightmare tokens that grow whenever something goes to exile, and threatens to exile an opponent's library outright. In the story Ashiok was beaten when Kellan held them still and Will hit them with a bolt of ice. Later someone used their face to free Eriette from her cell, and it was not them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"Most dreams reveal but fragments of what you could be. Only nightmares can show you who you truly are.\" —Ashiok",
                        "Prophetic Prism",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-troyan-and-beluna", "Troyan and Beluna",
                    listOf(
                        "Troyan is the set's visitor from somewhere else, and a sign of the age to come. He is a vedalken from Ravnica who had found his guild there limiting and dull. He was no brilliant scientist and did not care to be a test subject, but he could climb, and he won every spire climbing contest the city held. When the Omenpaths opened he walked through one to Eldraine to become an adventurer, and he plays the part of Jack in the set's take on Jack and the Beanstalk.",
                        "He guided Kellan and Ruby up the beanstalk to Stormkeld, and later he used the same route to steal treasure from the unsuspecting giants. {Troyan, Gutsy Explorer} taps for green and blue mana that can only be spent on big spells or spells with X in their cost, which is exactly the kind of plan a beanstalk climber would have. He was not in the first outline of the story. He was added when Kellan's role in the larger arc grew too big for Kellan to play Jack.",
                        "Beluna Grandsquall is the giantess he was robbing, leader of the storm giants of Stormkeld and, by coincidence, a kleptomaniac. She is slow to trust anyone, on the reasonable grounds that visitors might want to steal the treasures she stole first. {Beluna Grandsquall} makes adventure cards cheaper and her own adventure mills seven and keeps every adventure among them, a fitting card for a hoarder.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"The streets of Ravnica were stifling. Eldraine is an endless adventure paved in gold.\"",
                        "Troyan, Gutsy Explorer",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Set", "Enchanting Tales"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine, Episodes 1 to 5, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Ten Stories Tall, by Mark Rosewater, Making Magic on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Talion"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Troyan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-bargain", "Bargain",
                    listOf(
                        "Bargain is the set's new keyword, a fairy tale twist on kicker. As you cast a spell with bargain, you may sacrifice an artifact, an enchantment or a token, and if you do, the spell is bargained and does something more. It is the price you pay to a witch or a faerie for a better deal, and like every such price, it costs you something you already had.",
                        "The extra effect can be almost anything. {Torch the Tower} deals two damage, or three and a scry if bargained, and exiles whatever it kills. {Kellan's Lightblades} deals three to an attacker or blocker, or destroys it outright if bargained. Some bargains make the spell cheaper, like {Ice Out}, a counterspell that costs one less if bargained, and {Hamlet Glutton}. A few go further. {Lich-Knights' Conquest} lets you sacrifice any number of artifacts, enchantments and tokens and return that many creatures from your graveyard, and {Devouring Sugarmaw} demands a sacrifice every upkeep or it simply sits there.",
                        "Bargain works because the set is full of things to sacrifice. Food tokens, Roles, Treasure, rats and enchantments that already did their job when they entered are all fair fuel. The white and black archetype, built on Snow White, is the bargain and sacrifice deck, and it shows how the pieces fit, with enchantments like {Hopeless Nightmare} that hurt an opponent when they enter and then become a bargain later.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-celebration", "Celebration",
                    listOf(
                        "Celebration is an ability word for a party. A celebration ability turns on if two or more nonland permanents entered the battlefield under your control this turn. It does not care how many more than two, and it does not care whether they are still there. Tokens count, so a spell that makes a Food and a creature on the same turn is already a party.",
                        "Most celebration cards are red and white, the archetype built on Cinderella and the Grand Ball at Delverhaugh. {Gallant Pie-Wielder} has first strike, and double strike at a celebration. {Raging Battle Mouse} makes your second spell each turn cheaper and pumps a creature at the start of combat if you are celebrating. {Goddric, Cloaked Reveler} is a human noble with haste who becomes a four four flying dragon whenever there is a party, which is the story of the ball in a single rules box. {Ash, Party Crasher} grows a counter every time she attacks at a celebration.",
                        "Celebration rewards going wide fast, which is why the set gives the mechanic so many cheap ways to make two things at once, adventure creatures whose adventure side makes a token, Roles stapled to creatures, and the occasional {Stockpiling Celebrant} who bounces your own permanent so you can play it again and dance twice.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-roles", "Roles",
                    listOf(
                        "Roles are the set's new kind of token. Each one is a colourless Aura enchantment that enters attached to a creature and gives it a part to play in the story. A player can only have one Role of their own on a creature at a time. If you give a creature a new Role, your old one goes to the graveyard, though a creature can carry several Roles if different players put them there.",
                        "There are six in the set. The Cursed Role makes a creature a one one, a witch's curse for an opponent's best creature. The Monster Role gives plus one plus one and trample. The Royal Role gives plus one plus one and ward one. The Sorcerer Role gives plus one plus one and a scry every time the creature attacks. The Wicked Role gives plus one plus one, and when it goes to the graveyard every opponent loses a life. The Young Hero Role gives a creature a plus one plus one counter each time it attacks while its toughness is three or less. The Commander decks added a seventh, the Virtuous Role, which grows with every enchantment you control.",
                        "Because Roles are enchantments, and because replacing one sends the old one to the graveyard, they feed the rest of the set. {Monstrous Rage} pumps a creature and gives it a Monster Role, {Twisted Fealty} steals a creature and hands out a Wicked Role, and cards like {Ashiok's Reaper}, {Wicked Visitor} and {Savior of the Sleeping} all reward you whenever an enchantment of yours is put into a graveyard.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-adventures-again", "Adventures Again",
                    listOf(
                        "Adventures came back from Throne of Eldraine. An adventurer card has two halves, a permanent and a smaller instant or sorcery printed beside it. You can cast the adventure first, and when it resolves the card goes to exile instead of the graveyard, and later you can cast the permanent from exile. It is two spells from one card, the hero's adventure first and the hero after.",
                        "This time the adventurers went further. They are no longer only creatures. The mythic Virtues are enchantments with adventures, and some adventurers have an adventure side of a different colour, so a ten card cycle of rare creatures and another of uncommons each pair a creature of one colour with an adventure of another, like {Decadent Dragon}, a red dragon whose black adventure steals cards from an opponent's library. Even Kellan has one, and so does {Beluna Grandsquall}.",
                        "The set leans on the mechanic in other ways too. {Chancellor of Tales} copies every adventure you cast, {Storyteller Pixie} draws a card off each one, and {Edgewall Inn} sacrifices itself to bring an adventure card back. Blue and red, built on the Sorcerer's Apprentice, is the instants, sorceries and adventures deck, and its cards count adventures in your graveyard alongside spells.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-food-and-tapping", "Food and Frost",
                    listOf(
                        "Food returned too, the colourless artifact token you can pay two, tap and sacrifice to gain three life. This time it is more aggressive, with several legends built around it. The black and green archetype is the Food deck, told through Hansel and Gretel, where {Greta, Sweettooth Scourge} turns Food into counters and cards, {Tough Cookie} turns a Food into a four four, and the candy horrors of Sweettooth drop Food when they die. {The Goose Mother} lays it by the dozen and eats it to draw cards.",
                        "Other archetypes have their own pull. White and blue, the Snow Queen, taps the opponent's creatures and rewards you for it, with stun counters that keep them down and {Sharae of Numbing Depths} drawing a card when you do it. Blue and black is faerie control in the Sleeping Beauty mould. Black and red is a swarm of rats that cannot block. Red and green looks for creatures with power four or greater, the way Ruby does. Green and white is enchanted creatures, and green and blue, the Kingdom of Storms, wants big spells, rewarding every spell of mana value five or more.",
                        "Sagas round the set out, each one a fairy tale in three chapters. An uncommon cycle tells five of them, {The Princess Takes Flight}, {Gadwick's First Duel}, {The Witch's Vanity}, {Korvold and the Noble Thief} and {Welcome to Sweettooth}, and others like {Three Blind Mice} and {The Huntsman's Redemption} tell old stories in new ways.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine Release Notes, by Eric Levine, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Bargain"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Celebration"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Role"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-virtues", "The Virtues",
                    listOf(
                        "The five mythic Virtues are the set's centrepiece cycle. Each is an enchantment named for one of the five virtues of the Realm, with an adventure named for the court that held it. They are expensive as enchantments and cheap as adventures, so a Virtue is usually a useful spell early and a game winning engine later, and the card goes to exile in between to wait for you.",
                        "{Virtue of Loyalty} puts a +1/+1 counter on each of your creatures at your end step and untaps them, and its adventure, Ardenvale Fealty, makes a two two vigilant Knight at instant speed. {Virtue of Knowledge} makes abilities triggered by a permanent entering trigger an extra time, and its adventure copies an ability. {Virtue of Persistence} returns a creature card from any graveyard to the battlefield under your control every upkeep, and its adventure, Locthwain Scorn, gives a creature minus three minus three and gains you two life, so the same card is a cheap removal spell early and a graveyard thief late.",
                        "{Virtue of Courage} turns noncombat damage to an opponent into cards from the top of your library, and its adventure, Embereth Blaze, deals two to any target. {Virtue of Strength} makes each basic land you tap for mana produce three times as much, and its adventure, Garenbrig Growth, returns a creature or land card from your graveyard to your hand. In Commander the reach of the green and black ones grows with the table, one tripling every basic land you tap and the other able to take the best creature from any graveyard at the table, every turn.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-beanstalk-and-mirror", "Beanstalk and Mirror",
                    listOf(
                        "{Up the Beanstalk} is a two mana enchantment that draws a card when it enters and again whenever you cast a spell with mana value five or greater. In Standard that was a fair deal, since big spells are slow. In Modern it was not. The format is full of spells with high mana values that cost little or nothing to cast, and the Beanstalk let those decks draw a card off each one without losing any tempo. It was banned in Modern in December 2023.",
                        "{Beseech the Mirror} is the set's tutor. It searches your library for any card and exiles it face down, and if the spell was bargained you may cast the card for free if its mana value is four or less. Otherwise the card goes to your hand. It calls back to Indrelon, the magic mirror in Beluna's vault that told Kellan where to find a witch, and like the mirror it answers one question exactly, for a price.",
                        "{Agatha's Soul Cauldron} is another engine with a long reach. It exiles cards from graveyards, puts a counter on a creature whenever it exiles a creature card, and gives every creature you control with a +1/+1 counter the activated abilities of every creature card it has exiled. It also lets you spend mana as though it were any colour to activate abilities of your creatures. So any creature card with an activated ability, in any graveyard at the table, can become part of your plan, which is exactly what a witch's cauldron is for.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Beluna makes a habit of \"liberating\" valuables from the world below, from priceless artifacts to historic buildings.",
                        "Collector's Vault",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-standard-staples", "Spells of the Realm",
                    listOf(
                        "{Stroke of Midnight} destroys any nonland permanent at instant speed for three mana and leaves its controller with a one one Human in its place, Cinderella's carriage turned back into a pumpkin. It was a clean, flexible answer, and its flavour text, Ash's helmet clattering to a stop, is one of the best in the set. {Moonshaker Cavalry}, the ghostly army of High King Kenrith that followed him into the afterlife, gives every creature you control flying and a bonus equal to the number of creatures you control, the kind of card that ends a game the turn it lands.",
                        "{Lich-Knights' Conquest} costs a single black mana and turns a board of tokens, Roles and spent enchantments into a whole army back from the graveyard. {Monstrous Rage} is a one mana pump that leaves a lasting Monster Role behind. {Cruel Somnophage} grows with every creature card in every graveyard, and its adventure, Can't Wake Up, mills a player to feed it. {Expel the Interlopers} lets you choose a number from zero to ten and destroy every creature with at least that much power, a board wipe you can aim around your own team.",
                        "Then there are the lands. The Restless lands are a cycle of five enemy coloured lands that enter tapped and can become creatures. {Restless Cottage} becomes a four four Horror that makes Food and exiles a graveyard card when it attacks. {Restless Fortress} drains two each attack. {Restless Spire}, {Restless Bivouac} and {Restless Vinestalk} complete the cycle, and lands that are also threats always find a deck.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-legends-for-commander", "Legends for Commander",
                    listOf(
                        "Wilds of Eldraine is full of commanders. {Talion, the Kindly Lord} is a four mana blue and black faerie who names a number and draws you a card and costs an opponent two life each time that opponent casts a spell whose mana value, power or toughness matches it, a commander that lets a whole table play a guessing game against you. {Hylda of the Icy Crown} is the white and blue tapping commander, turning every tap effect into elementals, counters or cards.",
                        "{Agatha of the Vile Cauldron} makes the activated abilities of your creatures cheaper by her power, a natural engine for decks full of creatures with abilities to use. {The Goose Mother} lays Food and draws cards. {Johann, Apprentice Sorcerer} lets you look at the top card of your library at any time and cast one instant or sorcery from there each turn. {Yenna, Redtooth Regent} copies your enchantments, untapping herself whenever the copy is an Aura. {Lord Skitter, Sewer King} makes a rat every combat and eats graveyards when rats arrive. {Neva, Stalked by Nightmares} returns a creature or enchantment and grows every time an enchantment of yours goes to the graveyard.",
                        "The two colour signpost legends, ten uncommons from {Sharae of Numbing Depths} to {Troyan, Gutsy Explorer}, each point to one archetype and one tale, and every one of them can lead a deck. {Totentanz, Swarm Piper} makes a rat every time a nontoken creature of yours dies. {Syr Armont, the Redeemer} pumps every enchanted creature you control. {Greta, Sweettooth Scourge} turns Food into counters and cards. And {Eriette of the Charmed Apple} is built to lead an Aura deck that would rather nobody attacked it, draining the table a little more with every Aura it controls.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds Blue Yonder, Parts 1 and 2, by Mark Rosewater, Making Magic on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-enchanting-tales", "Enchanting Tales",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-bonus-sheet", "The Bonus Sheet",
                    listOf(
                        "Enchanting Tales is a bonus sheet that came with Wilds of Eldraine, in the same spirit as the Retro Artifacts sheet in The Brothers' War and Multiverse Legends in March of the Machine. It reprints sixty three enchantments, eighteen uncommons, thirty rares and fifteen mythics, in a special frame and under its own set code, WOT. Every Draft Booster held one in a slot of its own, so it never replaced another card. Set Boosters held one to three, and Collector Boosters three or four.",
                        "The cards are only legal where they were already legal, plus Historic on Arena, and they are not part of Standard, though they can be played in drafts and sealed events of the set. On Arena, where some of them were new, a handful were banned from Historic before they ever arrived, among them {Blood Moon}, {Necropotence}, {Sneak Attack} and {Land Tax}. They remained legal in Historic Brawl.",
                        "The mythic list reads like a wish list for a Commander player. {Rhystic Study}, {Smothering Tithe}, {Doubling Season}, {Parallel Lives}, {Necropotence}, {Omniscience}, {Bitterblossom}, {Grave Pact}, {Greater Auramancy}, {Defense of the Heart}, {Kindred Discovery}, {Repercussion}, {Land Tax}, {Blood Moon} and {Sneak Attack} were all mythic on the sheet, and the rares included favourites like {Sanguine Bond}, {Hardened Scales}, {Goblin Bombardment} and the five Leylines.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-the-books-of-eldraine", "The Books of Eldraine",
                    listOf(
                        "What makes Enchanting Tales special is not just which cards it holds. Its frame makes every card look like a page from an old book, and most of their new flavour texts are quotes from books and songs of Eldraine. The Wicked Slumber, Last Stand of the Realm, Tales of the Fae, Hexes of Dunbarrow, The Goose Mother and Other Tales, The Witch's Way and To Walk the Wilds are all quoted, and together they build a shelf of in world stories around the set.",
                        "Some fill in the main story. {Rest in Peace} quotes the king's order that the sleepers be laid in glass caskets. {Phyrexian Unlife} and {Spreading Seas} tell how Locthwain fell and how the hasty defence of Castle Vantress left it more moat than castle. {Fiery Emancipation} remembers Torbran striking the invaders like a bolt from the heavens. {Omniscience} lists Talion's titles in full. {Raid Bombardment} explains why the dwarves of Delverhaugh are called the Gluttoneers, and {Shared Animosity} reveals that their feud with the redcaps started over a spoon.",
                        "Others are tales of their own. Three Leylines follow the Nameless Knight of Summer, who passed a glowing spring, an enchanted glade and whispering lights and each time still walked on. {Curiosity} and {Rhystic Study} tell of Valtry, a merfolk girl of Loch Larent who collected things that fell to the bottom and dreamed of life on land. {Oppression} gives Lord Skitter's offer to Totentanz, and {Defense of the Heart} and {Parallel Lives} carry the curse of Redtooth Keep.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "\"They laughed with glee as the pixie spun gold from straw. They smiled and shrugged when their curtains and clothes were next on the wheel. Then he grabbed their hair.\"",
                        "Smothering Tithe, Enchanting Tales, quoting The Goose Mother and Other Tales",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-anime-borderless", "Anime Borderless",
                    listOf(
                        "Twenty of the sixty three Enchanting Tales cards also came in an anime borderless treatment, illustrated by Japanese artists and numbered on from the main sheet. They turned up in the same slot as other Enchanting Tales cards in some Wilds of Eldraine boosters. Japanese boosters carried Japanese language versions, and boosters in other languages carried them in English.",
                        "In Collector Boosters the anime cards could also appear in confetti foil, another twenty card run with glittering flecks across the art. The anime style reached past the bonus sheet too. Wizards Play Network stores gave out anime borderless versions of {Ice Out} and {Pyroblast}, illustrated by Takuma Ebisu, to celebrate local game stores alongside the set.",
                        "The anime treatment fell only on rares and mythics, five rares and fifteen mythics, so the cards most worth chasing on the sheet were the ones most likely to come drawn in a style nobody had seen them in before. Among them were {Greater Auramancy}, {Karmic Justice}, {Land Tax}, {Smothering Tithe} and {As Foretold}, old enchantments from Shadowmoor, Odyssey, Legends, Ravnica Allegiance and Amonkhet given a new look on a fairy tale world.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Enchanting Tales"),
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine"),
                StorySource("Story", "Depicting a Fairy-Tale Plane, See the Anime Borderless Cards included in Wilds of Eldraine, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Wilds of Eldraine, Enchanting Tales"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "woe-commander", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-virtue-and-valor", "Virtue and Valor",
                    listOf(
                        "Two Commander decks came out beside the set, led by Corey Bowen with Jeremy Geist. Each held a hundred cards, a display commander, a foil commander, two more foil legends, ten double sided tokens, a deck box, a life wheel and a two card Collector Booster sample. From these decks on, the packaging stopped showing the face commander on the front. The green and white deck was Virtue and Valor, led by {Ellivere of the Wild Court}, a knight who goes nowhere without her woodland squires.",
                        "Ellivere was born to a noble family of Ardenvale while the High King still ruled. She did not speak a word or cry until her tenth birthday, when, during a ceremony, she was meant to release a caged dove. The dove climbed onto her hand and sat there calmly, and only when she told it to fly did it go. Afterwards her mothers bought her pets from all over the Realm, and she freed each one soon after it arrived. When the invasion destroyed her home she wandered the Realm helping whoever she could, and Will Kenrith knighted her for it, the youngest knight there had ever been. Her first task was to go into the Wilds and look for a cure for the Slumber, where ouphes, bunnicorns, faeries and bumblesheep came out to greet her.",
                        "Her card makes a Virtuous Role, a new Role that grows with every enchantment you control, whenever she enters or attacks, and draws you a card whenever an enchanted creature of yours hits a player. The deck's new cards include {Gylwain, Casting Director}, {Giant Inheritance}, {Knickknack Ouphe}, {Liberated Livestock}, {Loamcrafter Faun}, {Ox Drover}, {Songbirds' Blessing}, {Timber Paladin} and {Unfinished Business}, around old Aura favourites like {Kor Spiritdancer} and {Mantle of the Ancients}, both with new art.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-fae-dominion", "Fae Dominion",
                    listOf(
                        "The blue and black deck was Fae Dominion, led by {Tegwyll, Duke of Splendor}. At a mere four hundred years old, Tegwyll is a newcomer to the politics of the High Fae in Tuinvale. Handsome and quick witted, he thought himself a natural fit for Talion's refined court, but his youth kept him on the edge of its long ceremonies, a sapling growing in the shade of the canopy. So he took his advantages where he found them, and he needed allies who could move unnoticed through the Wilds.",
                        "He found them in Alela, a lesser faerie and a schemer like himself, and in the mischievous faeries who served her in droves. The High Fae look down on their faerie cousins, which made them perfect eyes and ears. After years away, Tegwyll returned in a lavish coming of age ceremony escorted by flights of faeries, and the celebration lasted nearly a year, while he whispered in the ears of the most powerful fae. Those he could not win over, Alela and her gang ruined with pranks. His goal is Talion's throne, and he is willing to wait millennia for it.",
                        "His card is a flying deathtouch lord that gives other faeries +1/+1 and draws a card whenever one of them dies. Alongside him the deck brought new cards like {Alela, Cunning Conqueror}, {Tegwyll's Scouring}, {Shadow Puppeteers}, {Malleable Impostor}, {Archmage of Echoes}, {Blightwing Bandit}, {Nettling Nuisance} and {Faerie Bladecrafter}, and reprinted faerie classics like {Oona, Queen of the Fae}, {Glen Elendra Archmage}, {Sower of Temptation} and {Brazen Borrower}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "He weaves glamours as easily as breathing, his otherworldly beauty entrancing fae and mortal alike.",
                        "Tegwyll, Duke of Splendor",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "woe-courts-and-crowns", "Courts and Crowns",
                    listOf(
                        "Eight more cards carry the Commander decks' set code but are not in either deck. They were found only in Set and Collector Boosters of Wilds of Eldraine. Five of them are the Courts, one for each court of the Realm, rare enchantments that make you the monarch when they enter and then do something every upkeep, with a bigger effect while you are still the monarch.",
                        "{Court of Ardenvale} returns a cheap permanent from your graveyard each upkeep, to the battlefield if you wear the crown. {Court of Vantress} copies an artifact or enchantment. {Court of Locthwain} steals the top card of an opponent's library and lets the monarch cast it for free. {Court of Embereth} makes a three one Knight every upkeep and, for the monarch, deals damage to each opponent for every creature you control. {Court of Garenbrig} hands out counters and then doubles them for the monarch. The red Knight token for the Embereth court was never printed on paper.",
                        "The last three are {Throne of Eldraine}, a legendary artifact that taps for four mana of one colour for spells of that colour, and two mythic legends. {Brenard, Ginger Sculptor} turns your dying creatures into Food Golem copies of themselves, and {Korvold, Gleeful Glutton} is a cheaper Korvold for every type of permanent you sacrificed this turn, drawing cards and growing whenever he connects. All three are made for the command zone, not for the drafts they were opened in.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Wilds of Eldraine Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine Commander Decklists, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Wilds of Eldraine Commander packaging and inserts"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Wilds of Eldraine, Commander decks"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Ellivere"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Tegwyll"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
